From 7c78e5cd72f8e8a7c7ff48846e8e3bf5225475a2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:18:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 164/181] gsplat: composited splats in a TLAS of their own,
 S^-1 R^T precomputed

- OptiX: composited splat instances leave the scene's TLAS for one of
  their own (its handle in the padding after device_bvh), which the
  composite ray traverses alone.
- Each splat's ray-space transform S^-1 R^T is computed once per update
  (PointCloud::pack_gsplat_inverse(), a gsplat_inverse kernel array)
  instead of from the rotation and scale at every ray; opacity and
  radiance are read once. Moving splats keep the general path.

A production frame, 4.3 M splats, 3840x2560, 128 spp, RTX 3090:
138 s -> 131 s, image identical (0.1 % per block). Without splats the
frame takes 55 s: the splats' cost is the number of splats each ray
evaluates, which neither change touches. CPU and OptiX still match the
numpy reference.
